WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.060
Two million hectares of protected peat burned after adjacent drainage lowered water tables. This is unintended consequences, episode 181. In Ketapong Regency, West Kalimantan,

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:21.620
national zoning split each Peteland hydrological unit. Domes deeper than three meters were peat ecosystems with protection function. The shallow edges were cultivation function.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.960
The line was meant to separate conservation from cultivation.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.780
Oil palm canals went in on the permitted mineral soils and shallow peat.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.340
Those canals lowered the water table under the protected domes.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:37.060
Peat inside the protection lines dried, subsided, and burned.

00:00:38.174 --> 00:00:41.774
The officials who drew those maps were working after the fires of 2015.

00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:47.834
Those fires burned 2.6 million hectares across Indonesia, and one-third of that area was

00:00:47.834 --> 00:00:48.214
peat.

00:00:48.634 --> 00:00:54.454
The government created the Peatland Restoration Agency, BRG, and appointed Nazir Fowad

00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:55.294
as its chief.

00:00:55.754 --> 00:00:58.914
FOAD and the agency worked from rules already on the books.

00:00:59.474 --> 00:01:05.714
Ministry of Agriculture Decree No. 14 of 2009 already banned New Oil Palm on Pete That Deep.

00:01:06.374 --> 00:01:12.274
Government Regulation 71 of 2014, Caped Drainage Depth at 0.4 meters.

00:01:12.934 --> 00:01:20.074
BRG mapped 865 Pete Land hydrological units and required at least 30% of each unit, including

00:01:20.074 --> 00:01:22.934
the central domes, to remain in protection function.

00:01:23.514 --> 00:01:28.254
Aficials reason that plantations kept to the outer, shallower zones would leave the

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:31.954
carbon-rich domes wet and still allow economic use of the landscape.

00:01:32.534 --> 00:01:38.574
A peak conversion moratorium from 2011 and restoration targets set for 2020 followed

00:01:38.574 --> 00:01:39.474
the same logic.

00:01:39.994 --> 00:01:42.974
Draw a clear spatial line between protection and production.

00:01:43.614 --> 00:01:48.034
At the time, the maps and the hydrological unit framework appeared precise enough to

00:01:48.034 --> 00:01:49.854
enforce that separation on the ground.

00:01:49.854 --> 00:01:56.974
One objection at the planning stage asked whether water could move laterally across the boundary between shallow and deep zones.

00:01:57.414 --> 00:02:02.734
The prevailing view held that the depth rule, plus the drainage cap, would keep the dome saturated,

00:02:03.234 --> 00:02:08.574
because the pit itself was treated as a stable barrier once the permitted areas stayed within legal limits.

00:02:09.584 --> 00:02:16.604
Regency governments, including Ketapong, wrote the national zoning into local spatial plans through multi-stakeholder forums

00:02:16.604 --> 00:02:21.824
that brought plantation companies, small holders, and forestry agencies into the same room.

00:02:22.504 --> 00:02:28.464
Roughly half the map domes remained unplanted, so BRG pushed to designate those unplanted areas

00:02:28.464 --> 00:02:31.364
as protected even inside existing concessions.

00:02:31.864 --> 00:02:36.404
Companies on the permitted shallow fringes installed drainage canals and planted oil palm,

00:02:37.064 --> 00:02:40.044
following the water table limits in the 2014 regulation.

00:02:40.704 --> 00:02:45.524
Proponents pointed to the legal clarity of the protection function category and to the commitment

00:02:45.524 --> 00:02:51.484
to finish restoration by 2020. Some local officials noted that the policy preserves space for both

00:02:51.484 --> 00:02:57.484
conservation and continued cultivation within the same hydrological unit. The rollout treated each

00:02:57.484 --> 00:03:02.864
parcels drainage as an independent engineering choice. Canals were dug to the allowed depth on

00:03:02.864 --> 00:03:08.024
the cultivation side. The assumption was that any seepage would be minimal because the deeper

00:03:08.024 --> 00:03:14.064
peat held more water naturally. The meetings recorded agreement on the maps. Field verification of

00:03:14.064 --> 00:03:18.164
exact peat depths remained limited by the resolution of the surveys then available.

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:22.550
Drainage on the cultivation zones did not stop at the mapped boundary.

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:27.430
Water flowed outward through the porous peat and dropped the water table inside the protected

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:28.470
domes as well.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:34.610
In the X-MEGA rice project area, models showed that full drainage for oil palm would eventually

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:40.950
place 67% of the peat surface below flood level after 100 years because of subsidence.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:46.470
emissions. Each centimeter of water-table drawdown allows oxygen to reach peat that had been anaerobic.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:52.190
Oxidation then releases carbon dioxide and reduces the volume of the peat matrix by roughly

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:58.030
the same amount, so the surface lowers. Repeated cycles compound until the dome begins to flood

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:03.750
from below in wet seasons, while remaining dry enough to burn and dry seasons. Dry peat in

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:08.990
the domes ignited during the annual dry season, and fires cross back into the areas the zoning

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:15.070
had intended to keep wet. In 2016, Nazir Fowad observed that inaccurate maps still allowed

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:20.490
licenses on deep peat, so drainage continued under the legal cover of adjacent concessions.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:27.410
A 2017 study in wetlands ecology and management recorded 28% encroachment of plantations onto

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:33.050
protected peat despite that depth rule. Carbon emissions from the drained and burned peat reached

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:39.470
an estimated 350 to 400 million tons of carbon dioxide per year. Small holders and companies

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.990
that had followed the zoning found their fields threatened by the same fires the policy was meant

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:50.530
to prevent. Restoration by BRG, which relied on canal blocking, could not keep pace with ongoing

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:56.130
drainage from neighboring licensed areas. The boundary was hydrologically porous, so repeated

00:04:56.130 --> 00:05:01.930
fires erased the gain spatial separation had been meant to deliver. A further objection that better

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:06.470
enforcement of the drainage cap would solve the problem, ran into a harder fact.

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:11.690
Even legal drainage on one side of the line altered the hydraulic gradient across the entire

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:16.130
unit and pulled water from the dome faster than natural recharge could replace it.

00:05:16.993 --> 00:05:22.153
After the pattern became clear, BRG called for full protection of all remaining peat

00:05:22.153 --> 00:05:26.093
domes and for companies to forego replanting once the first cycle ended.

00:05:26.733 --> 00:05:31.833
Multi-stakeholder forums in Ketapong produced a local policy instrument aimed at reconciling

00:05:31.833 --> 00:05:36.873
protection and production, yet implementation stayed limited by mismatched rules across

00:05:36.873 --> 00:05:39.273
national, provincial, and district levels.

00:05:39.813 --> 00:05:44.793
Some plantations adopted zero-burning practices and tighter water management under guidance

00:05:44.793 --> 00:05:50.813
from the roundtable on sustainable palm oil, RSPO, but the underlying drainage continued

00:05:50.813 --> 00:05:54.213
wherever mineral soil or shallow peat permits remained valid.

00:05:54.753 --> 00:05:58.793
By most accounts, the restoration target for 2020 was not fully met.

00:05:58.793 --> 00:06:04.593
Yet, current efforts focus on aligning economic incentives and enforcing hydrological unit planning

00:06:04.593 --> 00:06:09.013
more strictly, yet the same tension between adjacent land uses persists.

00:06:09.633 --> 00:06:14.333
One practical adjustment under discussion is to treat the entire hydrological unit as

00:06:14.333 --> 00:06:19.973
the unit of permitting, rather than individual concessions, so that any new canal on the shallow

00:06:19.973 --> 00:06:23.853
fringe would require offsetting water table controls inside the dome.

00:06:23.853 --> 00:06:29.315
The lines around those deep domes rewarded any drainage that stayed just outside them,

00:06:29.715 --> 00:06:33.995
so water tables inside the protected zones fell even when the canals were legal.

00:06:34.715 --> 00:06:39.635
The conservation share required on each hydrological unit rewarded continued planting on the

00:06:39.635 --> 00:06:44.255
rest of the unit, with no mechanism to keep that planting from altering the whole unit's

00:06:44.255 --> 00:06:44.535
water.

00:06:45.195 --> 00:06:49.835
Applied separately to neighboring parcels, the depth rule and the drainage cap rewarded

00:06:49.835 --> 00:06:54.775
short-term planting decisions, whose subsidence and fire costs cross the parcel boundary.

00:06:55.215 --> 00:06:58.615
What was measured was compliance at the canal and at the map line.

00:06:59.095 --> 00:07:03.595
What moved was water through porous peat, faster than recharge could put it back.

00:07:04.115 --> 00:07:08.875
A depth cap on one concession cannot hold a dome if the next concession is free to steep

00:07:08.875 --> 00:07:09.555
in the gradient.

00:07:10.135 --> 00:07:14.975
A protection chair cannot stay saturated if the cultivated chair may drain the same peat

00:07:14.975 --> 00:07:15.295
body.

00:07:15.295 --> 00:07:20.715
Nazir Fowad and the Regency forums were using the separation the existing decrees made

00:07:20.715 --> 00:07:25.515
available, and that separation looked enforceable because the peat was treated as a barrier.

00:07:26.115 --> 00:07:27.515
The barrier was porous.

00:07:27.995 --> 00:07:32.075
Environmental intelligence is where this network stays with that regulatory pattern.

00:07:32.595 --> 00:07:35.975
A permit line laid across water that does not stop at the line.

00:07:36.475 --> 00:07:41.555
How might today's land use maps treat entire hydrological units as single decision spaces

00:07:41.555 --> 00:07:43.755
rather than collections of adjacent permits?

00:07:43.755 --> 00:07:45.615
That's the case for today.

00:07:46.135 --> 00:07:48.595
The lessons tend to travel further when you share them.

00:07:49.015 --> 00:07:53.175
So send this one to someone who'd find it useful and subscribe wherever you're listening.

00:07:53.715 --> 00:07:54.375
See you tomorrow.

